{"id":"extended-without-award","title":"Extended without a new award","question":"Which contracts and grants were carried on under the City’s rule for short extensions, with no new competition and no Board file?","finding":"58 contracts and grants worth $207.8 million are keyed under the purchasing rule for “short-term extensions”; $17.9 million has been paid under that rule since fiscal 2019, to 33 payees, 26 of them in two or more years. These are not short contracts: all 58 show a term longer than a year, typically 4.6 years from start to today’s end date, so the extension rule is the latest step in a long run. They sit with City Administrator (41); Public Health (4); Human Services Agency (4); Economic & Workforce Development (3). This is the only place the contract table marks an extension as such. For agreements large enough to need a Board vote, the site’s “The same winners, year after year” page lists 19 amended in three or more different years, and 232 contract numbers paid in every year from 2019 to 2026.","how":"Contracts in the Supplier Contracts table whose purchasing rule reads “SHORT-TERM CONTRACT EXTENSIONS (Chapter 21)” or “SHORT-TERM GRANT EXTENSIONS”, largest first, and payment lines keyed with the same rule in the payment file. Months are the recorded end date minus the start date. The chart and the count of payees paid in two or more years place each payment in the fiscal year its voucher number belongs to.","limits":"A short extension is a step under the purchasing chapter the rule names, and one obvious use is to keep a service running while a new competition is prepared; the amounts are small beside the City’s contracting as a whole. The contract table keeps no history: it shows today’s end date and amount, so it cannot say how many times any contract was extended, and an extension made by simply moving the end date leaves no mark at all. That is the main limit of this screen: “extended three or more times” can be counted only for agreements that came back to the Board, which the site’s “The same winners, year after year” page and the site’s “Audits and official documents” page already do. What would supply the rest: each department’s log of contract amendments. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"payee","t":"With","type":"org"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"kind","t":"Kind","type":"text"},{"k":"contract","t":"Contract","type":"text"},{"k":"number","t":"Contract number","type":"text"},{"k":"start","t":"Term starts","type":"date"},{"k":"end","t":"Term ends","type":"date"},{"k":"months","t":"Months","type":"int"},{"k":"agreed","t":"Agreed amount","type":"usd"},{"k":"paid","t":"Paid so far","type":"usd"},{"k":"nonprofit","t":"Nonprofit","type":"text"},{"k":"signs","t":"Warning signs elsewhere","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"department":"Human Services Agency","contract":"HSA Security Services 18-24","number":"1000010873","start":"2018-07-01","end":"2024-06-30","months":72,"agreed":24390196,"paid":21014715,"kind":"contract","nonprofit":"","payee":"Allied Universal  Security Services","payeeKey":null,"signs":5},{"department":"City Administrator","contract":"TC 77524 Electrical Supplies","number":"1000005805","start":"2017-07-05","end":"2024-06-30","months":84,"agreed":17446868,"paid":18063806,"kind":"contract","nonprofit":"","payee":"Buckles-Smith Electric Co","payeeKey":null,"signs":3},{"department":"City Administr